"The workload has no sole\u2011owner elements, so there is no function that uniquely requires any such element. The surface remains open because nginx shares the io_uring setup syscall (sc.io_uring_setup) to perform asynchronous I/O for high\u2011speed network traffic.  \n\nAn attacker who can reach this shared element gains two exploitation primitives: the ability to manipulate io_uring reference counts (leading to overflow or underflow) and to trigger a use\u2011after\u2011free condition in the io_uring subsystem. These primitives allow arbitrary kernel memory read/write and privilege escalation.  \n\nThe surface can be removed by disabling nginx\u2019s use of io_uring, for example by setting `use io_uring off;` in the nginx configuration or by building nginx without the io_uring module and relying on its default epoll event loop. This preserves the ability to serve HTTP requests while eliminating the sc.io_uring_setup exposure, though it reduces the asynchronous I/O throughput that io_uring provides."